"A veto can be overridden by two thirds of each chamber.": "b07bdf351068",
"A vetoed bill goes back to the chamber where it started. If two thirds of the members voting in each chamber, with a quorum present, vote to pass it again, it becomes law over the President's objections. That margin is hard to reach. According to the Senate's records, presidents have vetoed 2,599 bills since 1789, and Congress has overridden only 112 of those vetoes. Because overrides are rare, members who want a bill to become law usually try to address the President's objections earlier in the process, before the bill is sent to the White House.": "dc6c365f4dee",
"Any Member, Delegate or the Resident Commissioner from Puerto Rico in the House of Representatives may introduce a bill at any time while the House is in session by simply placing it in the ``hopper,'' a wooden box provided for that purpose located on the side of the rostrum in the House Chamber. Permission is not required to introduce the measure.": "1fed44f7cfe9",
"Any member of Congress may introduce a bill, and thousands do every term. In the 117th Congress, which met in 2021 and 2022, members introduced 15,058 bills and 176 joint resolutions. During the 117th Congress, 362 public laws were enacted. Most bills that are introduced never become law, and many of those never reach a vote on the floor. The road from an idea to a law is long, and the Constitution, the rules of each chamber, and the calendar of a two-year Congress all shape where a bill stops.": "1f3d005fad74",
"Any member of Congress may introduce a bill, and thousands do every term. In the 117th Congress, which met in 2021 and 2022, members introduced 15,058 bills and 176 joint resolutions. During the 117th Congress, 362 public laws were enacted. Most bills that are introduced never become law, and many of those never reach a vote on the floor. The road from an idea to a law is long, and the Constitution, the rules of each chamber, and the calendar of a two-year Congress all shape where a bill stops. The first and most common stopping place is a committee. Each chamber divides its work among standing committees: in the 119th Congress, the House has twenty and the Senate has twenty permanent committees. The committee chair decides which bills receive attention. A committee may hold hearings, rewrite a bill in a markup session, and vote to report it. It may also never act at all. In 1885 the political scientist Woodrow Wilson wrote that “Congress in session is Congress on public exhibition, whilst Congress in its committee-rooms is Congress at work.” He was describing the House of his day, but committees still decide which bills receive formal attention. In the House, a reported bill usually needs a special rule from the Rules Committee, which sets the length of debate and decides which amendments may be offered. The House then debates and votes; a majority of those voting is enough to pass it. A committee's silence is not always final. If 218 members, a majority of the whole House, sign a discharge petition, the bill can be brought to the floor without the committee's approval. Gathering that many signatures is difficult, but the possibility is part of the balance between a committee and the full House. The Senate works differently. Many bills pass by unanimous consent, with little or no debate, when no senator objects. But Senate rules allow debate to continue for a long time. To end debate on most bills over objection, supporters need cloture, which requires three fifths of the senators chosen and sworn: sixty votes when every seat is filled. Lesson 5 explained how that rule developed. In practice it means that many bills need support from more than a simple majority of senators before they can reach a final vote, even though the final vote itself needs only a majority. Passing both chambers is not enough. The House and the Senate must agree to exactly the same text, down to the last word. When their versions differ, the chambers may send amendments back and forth, which is officially called “amendments between the houses,” or appoint a conference committee of members from both chambers to write a compromise, called a conference report. Each chamber then votes on that report without changing it. Only when both have agreed to identical text is the final version printed as the enrolled bill, signed by the presiding officers of both chambers, and sent to the President. The President then has ten days, Sundays excepted, to act. Signing the bill makes it law. Returning it with objections is a veto. If the President does neither, the bill becomes law without a signature, unless Congress has adjourned and so prevented its return. In that case it does not become law, which is called a pocket veto, and Congress cannot override it. The House's own guide notes that Congress reads this power narrowly, as applying only when a Congress ends, and that the courts have not definitively decided how far it reaches. A vetoed bill goes back to the chamber where it started. If two thirds of the members voting in each chamber, with a quorum present, vote to pass it again, it becomes law over the President's objections. That margin is hard to reach. According to the Senate's records, presidents have vetoed 2,599 bills since 1789, and Congress has overridden only 112 of those vetoes. Because overrides are rare, members who want a bill to become law usually try to address the President's objections earlier in the process, before the bill is sent to the White House. The difficulty is deliberate. The framers built a system in which a law must win a majority in two chambers chosen in different ways and then win the President's signature or a supermajority. Supporters of the design say each stage catches mistakes and keeps laws stable. Critics say it lets a small number of people block what most want, and that problems go unaddressed while bills wait. Both sides agree on the facts: most bills never become law. Whether that is a strength or a weakness is the question in the design debate below.": "77444c4a2054",
